# Shared Project State

Work on this repo runs in many parallel lanes. Context fragmentation — each agent holding a
private, drifting snapshot of the project — has caused more damage here than any code defect:
duplicated investigations, two lanes clobbering one file, and fixes re-broken by a sibling who
did not know they existed.

**Three files are the shared memory. Read them before forming a theory or editing anything.**

- `docs/process/M1-DONE.md` — the acceptance conditions M1 must meet, each checkable, each
  marked met or not. This is the target. Work that does not move a line on it is not the
  priority, however satisfying it is.

- `docs/process/M1-STATUS.md` — what is fixed (with the commit), what is open (with why it is
  deferred), what has already been **disproven**, the regressions and the guard now catching
  each, and what every gate is structurally blind to.
- `docs/process/LANES.md` — which lane owns which files, the standing rules, the environment
  quirks, and what counts as evidence.

## Rules

- **Check `M1-STATUS.md` before investigating.** Several hypotheses have already been tested
  and disproven with measurements. Re-deriving one wastes a lane; contradicting one without
  new evidence is worse.
- **Check `LANES.md` before editing.** Do not edit a file it assigns to another lane. If the
  honest fix needs one, stop and report what you need — do not work around it.
- **Write findings back.** Anything discovered that outlives your task — a defect you did not
  fix, a stale comment, a gate blind spot, an invariant nothing pins, a disproven theory —
  belongs in `M1-STATUS.md`, or in your report if you do not own that file. A finding only you
  know is a finding that will be rediscovered or lost.
- **Correct these files when they are wrong.** They are written by fallible agents. An audit
  already refuted a load-bearing claim in `M1-STATUS.md`; the correction was more valuable than
  the original. Say what you changed and why.
- **Prefer deleting stale state to appending.** These files are useful only while they are
  short enough to be read. Fold resolved detail into a line; delete what no longer informs a
  decision.

## Trace, do not infer

State a mechanism only after reading the path the feature actually takes. Inferring
architecture from adjacent code has repeatedly produced confident, wrong diagnoses here — two
in one afternoon, each of which would have sent a worker at the wrong file, and one of whose
"fixes" would have made the defect worse. If you are handed a diagnosis, **verify it before
inheriting it**, and say so when it turns out to be stale. Correcting the brief is worth more
than executing it.

Two mechanical guards exist and are not suggestions: a `preToolUse` hook refuses writes to
another lane's files, to files shared by several lanes, and to the main checkout's source.
`.cursor/lane-ownership.json` is the enforced map. If a write is refused, that is the answer —
report what you need rather than routing around it.

## Verifying something visual

Judge an image by examining it, not by glancing at one wide frame. Crop and zoom to the thing
being claimed, take a second angle when the first is ambiguous, and iterate until the contact,
the seam or the surface is actually visible. A wide dark frame containing the subject somewhere
is not evidence — nine ladders were merged as "flush to the face" on exactly that, and one of
those captures turned out to be a blurred close-up of a wall with no ladder in it.

## Reporting a hunt or an audit

**Report everything you find, then rank it.** Do not suppress findings by severity while
hunting — filtering belongs in a separate pass, and a hunt that self-censors returns less than
it saw. Rank by what the finding was protecting or by how likely the owner is to hit it, and
say plainly which findings you are confident in and which need a human eye.

## Length

Match a document to what the task needs. Cover the substance and stop; no filler sections, no
restating the same finding in a summary, no boilerplate. The state files here are load-bearing
only while they are short enough to be read — prefer folding resolved detail into a line over
appending.

## Evidence standard

A claim in these files carries its evidence, or it carries an honest label saying it is
unverified. Do not launder a worker's report into a fact. "Merged" is not "works": a fix is
done when it is merged, gated, and recorded.
